hi there Steve Kaufman here again from  my yard here in Palm Springs  another less than like another ten days  or so we're back in Vancouver but today  I want to talk about something that may  discourage some of you and that is that  it takes a long time to learn a language  and I say this from experience because  if I look at the languages that I speak  well French you know we had French at  school we'll never learned it properly  but then I lived in France for three  years okay  and I have done a fair amount of a lot  of reading in French the Chinese I spent  a whole year or a little less than a  year but I was like six seven hours a  day and I read copiously if I look at  the languages that I've learned more  recently like Russian I mean I was years  at Russia and my statistics that link  tell me that I've read I don't know  three million words in Russian I've  listened to thousands of hours I don't  have the statistics in front of me but I  have spent a lot of time check was a  good year and I still might in my level  and check is not that good I was kind of  okay when I was there but and I  understand it well when I read it and I  hear it but to try and speak it would  take me a while to work it back up some  languages if they're related to ones  that you know go more quickly but still  Ukrainian was a full year and when I'm  away from it I lose it Greek you know I  went at it for six months I was doing  fine on Crete but now I you know it  would take me a bit of an effort to get  back to it I've been at Arabic for I  don't know nine ten months and I feel as  if I'm not making as much progress as I  would like except that I remember how  long I've spent on the other languages  and I realize that it takes a long time  now it depends on what your goals are  for me my goal in a language eventually  is to get what is called b2 in the  common European framework which is a  fair degree of fluency the ability to  understand a lot of stuff and this  simply takes a lot of time so what do we  do I mean this is an issue in teaching  languages in schools and so forth  how do  motivate people there's no instant  gratification that people have to be  somehow encouraged to put in the effort  to achieve a goal that's far away now it  depends how strongly you visualize that  goal when I started learning Russian I  was very motivated to be able to read  the classics of Russian literature in  the original by the way Master and  Margarita but not everyone is is  motivated that way but I remember  slogging through Tolstoy in the original  looking up every third word but being  excited at the fact that I was actually  reading Tolstoy in the original now that  makes me perhaps an outlier most people  would not be motivated by that but I  believe there are things we can do first  of all I think we have to move as soon  as possible  into content that's interesting so that  the actual learning activity becomes  enjoyable or interesting in some way I  think that's absolutely fundamental  that's more important than teaching  rules of grammar or anything else  testing people we have tests in school  or nobody can speak French at least in  Canada as a result of years and years of  traditional French instruction so we  have to make it interesting in some way  and then if it's like you know if you're  if you're an athlete if you're planning  to run a marathon that marathon might be  six months away but you're working out  every day and you feel good every time  you work out even the goal of becoming  more fit it's not something that you  measure you live on a day-by-day basis  but you feel good you people actually I  think enjoy the workout and they  particularly feel good when the workout  is over so somehow that daily learning  activity for language learning has to be  enjoyable and has to give people a sense  of satisfaction every time they do it  that way the fact that the goal the  final goal is actually quite far away  maybe a year maybe two years if the  learning activity is in some way  enjoyable or satisfying then people will  continue to do it  in that regard I think that what's  happening now on the Internet is very  exciting for example YouTube YouTube has  a lot of videos that have captions if  they have captions or subtitles then  that text is available for example at  link now we have an extension for  different browsers where you can simply  click on the extension and that video  the video the audio and the text are  automatically imported into link and you  can study them so you can study the  words and phrases you can listen to it  perhaps when you're not looking at it  and then you can actually look at the  video and try to understand so and if  it's on a subject that's of interest to  you how to fix your toaster a movie a  political discussion whatever it might  be you can then be doing that activity  that's interesting while at the same  time improving your language skills so  that's an example of making it enjoyable  while you're doing it so that to me the  goal in language instruction let me if  you always say well what are you doing a  classroom the kids aren't interested in  stuff somehow you have to find a way and  I the good teachers do this find a way  to make the actual learning activity  interesting and enjoyable or satisfying  because actually it takes a long time to  learn a language now if your goal is to  be able to say hello how are you and to  order some food maybe that can be done  with you know in a shorter period of  time but you really have no hold on that  language and you are quite defenseless  if the person comes back to you and says  you know other things beyond you know  where the bathroom is or whatever it is  you might have asked for so it takes a  long time to learn a language but there  are things that we can do that make our  language journey more enjoyable or if  you're a teacher I guess the challenge  is to make it more enjoyable for your  learners so that's what I have to say  today and I look forward to your  comments  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

